## Frequently Asked Questions

## Is There a Deadline on Lemon Law Claims?

Yes. Under lemon law in California, you have four years from the date you first discovered the defect to file a lemon law claim. Although this may seem like plenty of time, it’s important to act quickly. Waiting too long can make it more difficult to prove your case, and delays may suggest that the problem wasn’t significant enough to warrant immediate action.

## How Many Repair Attempts are Required to Qualify for a Lemon Law Claim?

The required number of repair attempts depends on the severity of the defect. For non-safety-related issues, manufacturers generally need to make at least two to four repair attempts. For serious defects that could pose safety risks, fewer attempts - sometimes just two - may be enough to qualify your vehicle for a lemon law claim.

## What Kind Of Defects Are Covered by California Lemon Law?

To qualify for a lemon law claim, the defect must significantly impair the vehicle’s use, safety, or value. This includes issues like engine problems, faulty brakes, transmission failures, or electrical malfunctions. Minor cosmetic defects are typically not covered, although there may be exceptions.

## Can I Keep Driving My Car While My Lemon Law Claim is Pending?

Yes, you can continue to drive your vehicle while your lemon law claim is in progress, as long as it’s safe to do so. But if the defect makes your car unsafe or unreliable, it’s best to avoid using it until the issue is resolved.

## What Happens if the Manufacturer Refuses to Settle My Lemon Law Claim?

If a manufacturer refuses to settle, your California lemon law attorney can escalate the case to arbitration or court. Most manufacturers prefer to settle before this point to avoid the time and cost of a trial, but if necessary, your lawyer will go the distance for a fair outcome.
